---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout title="Job Fit Agent — Design Decisions">
  <article class="project">
    <header class="hero">
      <p class="crumb"><a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}projects/job-fit-agent`}>Job Fit Agent</a></p>
      <h1>Design decisions</h1>

      <nav class="actions">
        <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}projects/job-fit-agent/learnings`} class="btn">Learnings</a>
        <span class="btn current">Design decisions</span>
        <a href="https://github.com/alawal1/job-fit-agent" class="btn">⌥ Code</a>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <section>
      <h3>Judgment goes to the LLM; classification goes to code.</h3>
      <p>Deciding whether "some analytics exposure" is a real gap is a judgment call — that's the LLM's job. Deciding whether "communication" is a soft skill is mechanical — that belongs in Python. Mixing the two produces inconsistent verdicts on identical inputs.</p>

      <h3>The profile is the source of truth — and it has to be honest.</h3>
      <p>An early version gave a "high confidence apply" verdict on a role that was actually a clear skip. The bug wasn't in the prompt — it was in the profile data. The agent believed the flattering version. See <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}projects/job-fit-agent/learnings`}>Learnings</a> for the full story.</p>

      <h3>Structured outputs, not free text.</h3>
      <p>Every tool returns a JSON object against a fixed schema. The verdict is an enum, not a sentence. This makes the agent debuggable: when a verdict is wrong, I can inspect the signals and see whether the reasoning was bad or the input was.</p>

      <h3>Filters before judgment.</h3>
      <p>Hard filters (language, seniority) run before any soft-fit assessment. There's no point asking the LLM to weigh trade-offs on a job that fails a hard constraint — and doing so wastes tokens and invites hallucinated reasoning.</p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>
